> // dummy interface to "confuse" type resolution

Why would you try to confuse type resolution? 

Below seems enough for reprouction.

```java
class MergeListTest {
    private static class MyArrayList<T> extends ArrayList<T> {
        static int constructorCount = 0;
        public MyArrayList() {
            if (constructorCount > 0) {
                throw new Error("Should only be called once");
            }
            ++constructorCount;
        }
    }

    public static class MergeList {
        @JsonMerge
        @JsonProperty
        List<String> values = create();

    }

    public static List<String> create() {
        List<String> list = new MyArrayList<>();
        list.add("a");
        return list;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        JsonMapper MAPPER = JsonMapper.builder().build();
        MergeList mergeList = new MergeList();

        // Fails with exception here
        MergeList w = MAPPER.readValue(("{\"values\":[\"x\"]}"), MergeList.class);
    }
}
```

@JooHyukKim There is initialization block:

```
    public static class MergeList {
        @JsonMerge
        @JsonProperty
        List<String> values = create();

    }
```

which initializes `value` when `MergeList` is created by `ObjectMapper` (via regular `BeanDeserializer` constructed for `MergeList`).

So I don't think "updatingReader" should be needed here.

Looking at `CollectionMergeTest` it seems there is exact test:

```
    @Test
    public void testCollectionMerging() throws Exception
    {
        CollectionWrapper w = MAPPER.readValue(a2q("{'bag':['b']}"), CollectionWrapper.class);
        assertEquals(2, w.bag.size());
        assertTrue(w.bag.contains("a"));
        assertTrue(w.bag.contains("b"));
    }

    static class CollectionWrapper {
        @JsonMerge
        public Collection<String> bag = new TreeSet<String>();
        {
            bag.add("a");
        }
    }
```

with one difference being that Collection is of known JDK type, not custom one.

So I wonder if/suspect that custom subtype is significant here.

I never used `readerForUpdating`. My use case is rather straightforward. I have a data object that holds various data types, and one of them is an `interface MyList<T> extends List<T>` which is initialized on construction, as can be done with JDK's `List<T>`. When I deserialize, because `List` is a known JDK collection, Jackson knows what to do under the hood:

```java
class MergeList {

	interface MyList<T> extends List<T> {}
	private static class MyArrayList<T> extends ArrayList<T> implements MyList<T> {}
	static <T> MyList<T> create() {
		return new MyArrayList<T>();
	}

	@JsonMerge
	@JsonProperty
	List<String> values = create(); // known collection

	public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
		JsonMapper MAPPER = JsonMapper.builder().build();
		MergeList mergeList = new MergeList();
		var string = MAPPER.writeValueAsString(mergeList);
		MergeList w = MAPPER.readValue(string, MergeList.class);
	}
}
```
completes successfully. Changing `List` to `MyList` for `values` throws the deserialization exception, even though Jackson can check that `MyList` *is* a `List`. This is the perplexing part. If Jackson looks for known types by `x.getClass() == List.class` then this discrepancy would happen, but using `Class::isInstance` or `Class::isAssignableFrom` should expand the default handling to any subclass/interface. I assume there's also code for calling `add` and `put` on these known collection for the process of merging, they would be called the same way also for subtyped user-made collections.

So to answer @JooHyukKim:
> How would Mapper would update existing list if there is no shared connection between the original object and deserialization?

However is works already with `List`.
